WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.260
Cancer is a genetic disease.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.420
But most changes in a tumor are not inherited.

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.240
They arise in body cells during life.

4
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.560
Some changes switch on genes that drive growth.

5
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.320
Others switch off genes that restrain it.

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.100
These changes matter because cells divide more or die less, and their descendants

7
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.100
expand.

8
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.480
An oncogene is an accelerator stuck on.

9
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.320
One altered copy is usually enough.

10
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.660
A tumor suppressor is a brake.

11
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.980
One working copy usually holds, so both copies normally must lose function.

12
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.700
These are useful rules, with exceptions.

13
00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:44.103
A tumor is usually a clone, the descendants of one cell.

14
00:00:44.963 --> 00:00:46.143
More changes accumulate.

15
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:49.403
A driver gives its cell a selective advantage.

16
00:00:50.323 --> 00:00:54.163
A passenger gives no advantage and rides along as the clone expands.

17
00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:56.563
Mutation supplies variants.

18
00:00:57.943 --> 00:01:00.283
Selection favors those that divide more or die less.

19
00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:05.403
A resistant subline can survive treatment and expand, so treatment can fail.

20
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:09.713
RB1 shows how inherited risk fits this picture.

21
00:01:10.613 --> 00:01:13.433
A child inherits one broken copy in every cell.

22
00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:16.553
Yet only a few retinal cells become tumors.

23
00:01:17.613 --> 00:01:21.493
Why? One working copy usually still provides the brake.

24
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:24.787
The inherited change is the first hit.

25
00:01:25.587 --> 00:01:28.547
A rare second hit removes the working copy's function.

26
00:01:29.447 --> 00:01:33.767
Often this is loss of heterozygosity: the cell keeps only the mutant allele.

27
00:01:34.807 --> 00:01:39.867
Deletion, chromosome loss or mitotic recombination can supply that second hit.

28
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.200
Without a germline first hit, both hits must arise in the same body cell.

29
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.760
These tumors tend to be single, one-sided and diagnosed later.

30
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:55.253
So the inherited allele is dominant in a family but recessive in the cell.

31
00:01:56.113 --> 00:01:57.333
A pedigree tracks risk.

32
00:01:58.233 --> 00:01:59.873
A cell needs both copies disabled.

33
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:03.333
Genome instability adds variation.

34
00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:06.873
Faulty DNA repair can increase mutations.

35
00:02:08.013 --> 00:02:11.813
Faulty chromosome segregation produces chromosome gains and losses.

36
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:15.513
That variation supplies more material for selection.

37
00:02:16.617 --> 00:02:21.457
Sequencing a tumor and matched normal tissue helps separate somatic from germline

38
00:02:21.457 --> 00:02:24.897
changes. Variants in both are usually germline.

39
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:28.417
Variants only in the tumor are usually somatic.

40
00:02:29.137 --> 00:02:33.917
The tumor sample also contains normal cells, so its purity matters when reading

41
00:02:33.917 --> 00:02:34.777
variant fractions.
